Here's a Terraform configuration that defines an IAM policy, an IAM role, and an EKS pod identity association.

**Note:** This configuration assumes you have the necessary AWS credentials configured for Terraform. You will also need to replace the placeholders in the configuration (e.g. `example-cluster`, `example-namespace`, etc.) with your actual cluster and namespace names.

**main.tf**
```terraform
# Configure the AWS provider
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

# Define the IAM policy for pod identity
resource "aws_iam_policy" "example" {
  name        = "example-pod-identity-policy"
  description = "Policy for pod identity management"

  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = [
          "sts:AssumeRole",
        ]
        Effect = "Allow"
        Resource = ["*"]
      },
    ]
  })
}

# Define the IAM role for pod identity
resource "aws_iam_role" "example" {
  name        = "example-pod-identity-role"
  description = "Role for pod identity management"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "eks.amazonaws.com"
        }
      },
    ]
  })
}

# Attach the S3 read-only policy to the IAM role
resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "example" {
  role       = aws_iam_role.example.name
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess"
}

# Define the EKS cluster
resource "aws_eks_cluster" "example" {
  name     = "example-cluster"
  role_arn = aws_iam_role.example.arn

  # Skipping the version as it defaults to the latest available version
}

# Define the EKS node group
resource "aws_eks_node_group" "example" {
  cluster_name    = aws_eks_cluster.example.name
  node_group_name = "example-node-group"
  node_role_arn   = aws_iam_role.example.arn

  # Skipping the version as it defaults to the latest available version
}

# Define the EKS pod identity association
resource "aws_eks_pod_identity_association" "example" {
  cluster_name = aws_eks_cluster.example.name
  namespace    = "example-namespace"
  service_account = "example-service-account"

  role_arn = aws_iam_role.example.arn
}
```

This Terraform configuration defines:

* An IAM policy (`example`) that grants EKS pods the ability to assume a role.
* An IAM role (`example`) that uses the `example` policy to allow EKS pods to assume this role.
* An IAM role policy attachment (`example`) that attaches the `AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess` policy to the `example` role.
* An EKS cluster (`example`) that uses the `example` role.
* An EKS node group (`example`) that uses the `example` role.
* An EKS pod identity association (`example`) that links the `example` role to a specific EKS cluster, namespace, and service account.

You can apply this configuration to your AWS account using the `terraform apply` command. Make sure to replace the placeholders with your actual cluster and namespace names.

