The following Terraform program will create an S3 bucket named "mybucket" with object lock enabled in the us-west-2 region. It will also create an S3 bucket policy to enable object lock at the bucket level. The bucket policy will be attached to the new bucket.

**Terraform HCL Program:**
```hcl
# Configure the AWS provider
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

# Create a new S3 bucket with object lock enabled
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "mybucket" {
  bucket = "mybucket"
  acl    = "private"

  # Enable object lock
  versioning {
    enabled = true
  }

  # Enable object lock configuration
  object_lock_configuration {
    enabled = true
  }
}

# Create an S3 bucket policy to enable object lock
resource "aws_s3_bucket_policy" "mybucket" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.mybucket.id

  # Policy to enable object lock
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Sid       = "ObjectLockPolicy"
        Effect    = "Allow"
        Action    = "s3:GetObject"
        Resource  = aws_s3_bucket.mybucket.arn
        Condition = {
          Null   = {
            "s3:x-amz-bucket-key-enabled": "true"
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  })
}
```
This Terraform program defines two resources:

1. `aws_s3_bucket`: Creates an S3 bucket named "mybucket" with object lock enabled.
2. `aws_s3_bucket_policy`: Creates an S3 bucket policy to enable object lock at the bucket level.

The `aws_s3_bucket_policy` resource attaches the object lock policy to the new S3 bucket. The policy is defined as a JSON string using the `jsonencode` function.

